h1('How To Tell If It\'s Working')
para("Commit to 10 videos. Post once a week with the same formula, and don't judge it until you've done 10. RP's results came from over 30 uploads.")
para('Then compare the median views of your next 10 videos with your last 10. You don\'t need anything fancier than that to start.')
h2('Is Your Thumbnail The Problem?')
para('When a video flops, check the six parts first. This is the newest upload on RP\'s channel:')
img('miami.jpg', 4.5)
para('A four-panel collage with a beach, a pool, a Rolls and "my fan" with an arrow. Not one candle on it. It did about a quarter of what a formula video does.')
para("The diagnosis took four seconds. It's a thumbnail problem, not a content problem, because the video underneath it is good. That's what a written formula buys you. Channels without one get a bad upload and then spend a week arguing about whether the audience changed.")
para("If you want a number to look at, check the video's click-through rate against your normal. I'll show you exactly how in the next section.")


# ================= TESTING =================
h1('How To Test Your Thumbnails')
para("Once your formula is in place, testing is how you squeeze more views out of every video. It can bring a video that flatlined back to life, and it can push a video that's already doing well even further.")
para("But if you screw this up, you can kill a good video's growth. Test the wrong things at the wrong time and you take a video that had momentum and stall it. So here's how we do it for our clients, minus the button-by-button stuff.")
h2('Step 1: Know Your Own Numbers First')
para("Two numbers matter here. **Click-through rate (CTR)** is the percentage of people who click after they see your thumbnail. **Average view duration (AVD)** is how long they watch once they click. You'll find both in YouTube Studio under Analytics.")
para("People always ask me what a good CTR is. The only benchmark that matters is your own channel's. Most channels sit somewhere between 2% and 6%, but a 4% on your channel could be a great video or a bad one depending on your normal.")
img('ctr_benchmarks.jpg', 4.0, caption='(Most channels land between 2% and 6%. Yours is the only number that counts.)')
para("So go to your last 10 videos and write down each one's CTR at three points: 24 hours after upload, 7 days after, and 30 days after. Then average each column. CTR drops as a video gets older and gets shown to colder audiences, which is why you need all three.")
para("Say you end up with 14% at 24 hours, 9% at 7 days and 7% at 30 days. Now you have benchmarks. Any video sitting above them gets left alone. Any video sitting below them is a candidate for a test. If you haven't posted 10 videos yet, use 5% as a rough line.")
h2("Step 2: Don't Pick The Winner On Clicks Alone")
para("This is the mistake I see the most. Someone runs a test, sees one thumbnail got the highest CTR, and calls it the winner.")
para("The problem is that a thumbnail can get a ton of clicks because it promises something the video doesn't deliver. People click, realise it's not what they expected, and leave. YouTube sees them leave and stops pushing the video. So you win the test and lose the video.")
para("What you want is the thumbnail that gets the click **and** keeps people watching. The simple way to score it is to multiply CTR by average view duration. For example, 6.07% CTR with a 369-second average view duration gives you a score of about 22,398 (6.07 x 369 x 10). The variation with the highest score wins, even if it isn't the one with the highest CTR.")
img('yt_ab_report.jpg', 5.0, caption="(YouTube's own test report splits the result by watch time, not clicks. Same idea.)")
para("And ignore total views when you compare versions. The first thumbnail in a test usually gets a bump from subscriber notifications, so it'll look better on views even when it isn't.")
h2('Step 3: Follow These Testing Rules')
bullets(["Always keep the original in the test. Without it you might find the best of your new versions and never notice the original was better than all of them.",
         "Test one thing at a time. Thumbnail first, because it usually moves CTR the most. If the original thumbnail keeps winning and the video is still underperforming, the thumbnail probably isn't the problem, so test the title next. Only test a new title and thumbnail together when both of those have failed.",
         "Test big swings, not small tweaks. A different shirt colour isn't worth a test. A different number, a different image of your buyer's world or a different 2 or 3 words is.",
         "Stay inside your formula. Every version you test should still look like your channel. You're testing the variable parts, not a whole new style.",
         "Run tests when your audience is awake. If one version only gets shown at 3 AM and another gets shown at lunch, the test isn't fair.",
         "Make sure the thumbnail matches the video. A thumbnail that over-promises might win on clicks and then lose everything after the click."], numbered=True)
img('which_one.jpg', 5.0)
h2('Step 4: Test New Uploads Fast')
para("The first 24 to 48 hours of a video matter the most, so you don't want to spend a whole day showing a weaker thumbnail.")
para("Before you upload, make 4 or 5 versions inside your formula and upload with the one you think is best. Then rotate all of them, original included, about one hour each, during the hours your audience is most active. After one full rotation you'll usually see a clear difference. If one version is clearly ahead on CTR and watch time, stop the test and lock that thumbnail in so YouTube has one consistent thumbnail to push.")
para("YouTube Studio's built-in Test & Compare is free and lets you test up to 3 thumbnails, but it runs over days, not hours. For hourly testing with more versions, we use thumbnailtest.com. If you're just starting out, the free option is fine.")
img('test_compare.jpg', 4.0, caption="(YouTube Studio's built-in test. Free, up to 3 thumbnails.)")
para("If the video still isn't where you want it after 48 hours, run a second round with new versions or move on to testing the title.")
h2('Step 5: Test Old Videos Slow')
para("This is the part most people skip, and it's free views. Your old videos aren't dead. YouTube keeps recommending videos for years, so a better thumbnail can bring an old video back.")
para("Three kinds of old videos are worth testing:")
bullets(["Videos with good content that never got the views they deserved.",
         "Videos that are already in your top 10. A small improvement on a video that already gets a lot of impressions adds up to a lot of extra views.",
         "Videos with something out of date on the thumbnail, like last year's date, an old version of a tool, or you looking noticeably different."])
para("For old videos, go slow. Make 3 to 5 new versions, test them one day at a time for about two weeks, and keep the original in. Then pick the winner on CTR times watch time, and only switch if it clearly beats the original.")
para("One thing to watch: if you've changed how you look, don't put today's face on a video from three years ago. People click, see someone who looks different, and leave. Update the text, the image of your buyer's world or the background, and keep a photo of you from around when you filmed it.")
h2('Step 6: Make It A Weekly Habit')
para("The channels that get the most out of this don't test once. They check every week. Here's the routine we run for clients:")
bullets(["Once a week, look at your videos against your 24-hour, 7-day and 30-day benchmarks.",
         "Make a short list: new videos below benchmark, older videos with potential, top performers that could do even better, and anything with outdated thumbnails.",
         "Test them using the rules above.",
         "After you switch to a winner, check it again in 1 to 2 weeks and compare before and after.",
         "Keep a simple log of what you changed and what happened. After a few months, it tells you exactly what your audience clicks on."], numbered=True)
para("Do this every week and your back catalogue keeps getting more views without you making a single new video.")

